{"title":"On Bergman’s *Persona*","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/971678813/0/marginalrevolution~On-Bergmans-Persona.html","date":1792133592000,"content":"<blockquote><p>I first saw Ingmar Bergman’s <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://player.bfi.org.uk/subscription/film/watch-persona-1966-online\"><em>Persona</em>,</a> his stone-cold classic of 1965, as a teenager, and God knows how many times I have seen it since. It has the greatest opening sequence in cinema: a percussive, rapid-fire montage that can never be wholly accommodated or understood. The film’s story is, however, a simple one (even if its Jungian resonances are deep and complex). A celebrated actress, Elisabeth Vogler (one of <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.imdb.com/name/nm0880521/\" target=\"_blank\">Liv Ullmann’s</a> defining roles), is struck dumb, having dried up in the middle of a performance of <em>Electra</em>. Three months later, still speechless, she is granted a nurse, Alma (<a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.imdb.com/name/nm0000761/\" target=\"_blank\">Bibi Andersson</a>), who, fearing silence, cannot stop talking to and at her patient. Elisabeth becomes the audience, Alma the performer, and the two personalities intertwine against a biblical monochrome landscape of sand, rock and water. More than 60 years on, it remains inexhaustible, never to be entirely absorbed, intimate, stark and utterly compelling.</p></blockquote>\r\n<p>Here is <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://engelsbergideas.com/notebook/persona-ingmar-bergmans-stone-cold-classic/\" target=\"_blank\">more from Paul Lay</a>.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/10/on-bergmans-persona.html\">On Bergman’s *Persona*</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/971678813/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/971678813/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/fblike20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/29/971678813/marginalrevolution,\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/pinterest20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/24/971678813/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/x.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/19/971678813/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/email20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/20/971678813/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/rss20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/10/on-bergmans-persona.html#comments\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/comments20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/10/on-bergmans-persona.html/feed\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/commentsrss20.png\" /></a> \r\n<div><a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/10/on-bergmans-persona.html#comments\"><h3>Comments</h3></a><ul><li><a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/10/on-bergmans-persona.html#comment-161082929\">The 1955 film adaptation of Oklahoma! 1955 (Rogers and ...</a> <i>by 10 years earlier</i></li><li><a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/10/on-bergmans-persona.html#comment-161082925\">Spoilers?</a> <i>by Hadur</i></li><li><a href=\"https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/10/on-bergmans-persona.html#comment-161082920\">In reply to Biggles f'Tang.   In the old days, y